<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="audit-and-siem"
      updated="August 4, 2026"
      source_path="portal/apps/emisar_web/lib/emisar_web/controllers/marketing_html/docs_audit.html.heex"
      toc={[
        {"recorded", "What gets recorded"},
        {"reading", "Reading it in the dashboard"},
        {"exporting", "The export API"},
        {"polling", "A poller that keeps its place"},
        {"health", "Watch the poller itself"},
        {"alerting", "What to alert on"},
        {"retention", "Retention"},
        {"journal", "The runner-side journal"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="audit-and-siem">
        <:dek>
          Everything that matters leaves a record: every dispatch and its outcome, every approval
          decision, every policy edit, every credential minted or revoked, every sign-in — even
          the failed ones.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/audit-view.webp"
        title="emisar console · Audit log"
        caption="Every dispatched action with its actor, target runner, outcome, source IP, and time — filterable, and exportable to your SIEM."
        alt="The Audit log in the dashboard: a chronological feed of dispatched actions, each with its actor, target runner, outcome (succeeded, failed, errored), source IP, and timestamp — filterable, and exportable to a SIEM."
      />

      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="siem"
        copy_text={
          ~s(curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $AUDIT_KEY" https://emisar.dev/api/audit?since=2026-07-23T00:00:00Z)
        }
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $AUDIT_KEY" \
    https://emisar.dev/api/audit?since=2026-07-23T00:00:00Z
{~s({"id":"019f8a5f-7759-7a33-a47e-9a7c28e10bd1","request_id":"req_cF-s3B3Z-Iu38MDhBCdqlw","account_id":"019f8a5f-74c7-7b9a-980a-0e00422d2541","actor_kind":"user","actor_label":null,"actor_id":"019f8a5f-749b-72b9-b70f-04b56ebf9e83","ip_address":null,"event_type":"action_run.pending_approval","occurred_at":"2026-07-22T15:09:00.121466Z","target_id":"019f8a5f-75b2-7a64-a955-152da6a993f3","mcp_client_metadata":null,"user_agent":null,"payload":{"action":"postgres.reload_conf","executed_command_truncated":false,"run_id":"019f8a5f-7758-7cfc-8790-77c9aa9400fd"},"target_label":"pg-primary-iad","target_kind":"runner"})}</.docs_code>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="One event per line, forward-only">
        Every line is a complete JSON object — the <code class="text-zinc-300">event_type</code>, the
        <code class="text-zinc-300">actor_id</code>
        / <code class="text-zinc-300">target_id</code>
        to correlate on, the request metadata (<code class="text-zinc-300">ip_address</code>, <code class="text-zinc-300">request_id</code>), and the full <code class="text-zinc-300">payload</code>.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="recorded">What gets recorded</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Events are named
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">domain.action</code>
        — <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">action_run.denied</code>
        (a dispatch policy rejected, with the reason), <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">approval.approved</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">policy.updated</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">membership.role_changed</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">user.sessions_revoked</code>, …
        over sixty types across runs, approvals, policies, packs, runners, credentials, team,
        and account changes. Each carries the actor (operator, API key, or runner), the
        affected entity, a structured payload (a policy edit records its exact diff), and an
        account-scoped timestamp. Audit rows are written in the
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">same database transaction</strong>
        as the change they
        describe — an action that committed has its audit row, always.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="reading">Reading it in the dashboard</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <a
          href={~p"/app/audit"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Audit</a>
        filters by event type or actor kind; every
        row expands to the full payload, and references resolve to live labels (the runner's
        name, the user's email) so you're not cross-referencing UUIDs. LLM-driven runs carry
        their attribution — which client, which key, which session — plus the required <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reason</code>, so you can answer what the
        agent did last night, and why, with a single filter.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="exporting">The export API</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Two ways out: the <strong>Export CSV</strong>
        button on the
        Audit log downloads exactly the view you've filtered to, and
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">GET /api/audit</code>
        serves NDJSON — one event per line, forward keyset order by <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">occurred_at</code>, then id.
        Mint a read-only <strong>audit-export</strong>
        token from <a
          href={~p"/app/audit/export"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Audit export</a>; that token
        can read events and nothing else — it's a separate credential kind from an MCP key, so
        it can never list runners or execute an action. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="emisar does not push events.">
        There is no managed log sink, webhook, or streaming transport. A SIEM with a cursor-aware
        HTTP input can poll this endpoint directly; otherwise a collector polls emisar and forwards
        what it reads. Collector is a role, not another service you have to run — it can be your
        SIEM's built-in HTTP input, a log agent you already run, or a small scheduled poller. Either
        way the delay between an action and its appearance in your SIEM is your poll interval and
        the rate limit below, not real time.
      </.docs_callout>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># first pull — everything since a timestamp</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -s <span class="text-brand-300">"https://emisar.dev/api/audit?since=2026-06-01T00:00:00Z&amp;limit=500"</span> \
    -H <span class="text-brand-300">"Authorization: Bearer $AUDIT_KEY"</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500"># follow-ups — same filters, plus the cursor the previous response returned</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -s <span class="text-brand-300">"https://emisar.dev/api/audit?limit=500&amp;cursor=$NEXT"</span> \
    -H <span class="text-brand-300">"Authorization: Bearer $AUDIT_KEY"</span></.docs_code>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Start once, then always resume.</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">since</code>
            is an inclusive lower bound on
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">occurred_at</code>
            and belongs only to the first call; omitting it reads from the start of your retained
            history. Once you hold a cursor, send
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">cursor</code>
            — it wins over <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">since</code>
            and resumes strictly after the event it names, so a poll can't rewind by accident.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Two headers, two different jobs.</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">X-Next-Cursor</code>
            is your resume point — the last row on the page — and rides every page that returned
            events, full or short. The RFC 5988
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
              Link: &lt;…&gt;; rel="next"
            </code>
            rides only a <em>full</em>
            page and means more may be available right now; it is not a promise that another event
            exists. Follow it while pages come back full, then go back to your interval.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">An empty page is an answer.</strong>
            It sets neither header. Keep the cursor you already had and poll again later — there is
            nothing to advance past.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Keep the filters identical for the life of a cursor.</strong>
            A cursor is an opaque position in the ordering — it carries no account, no filter set,
            and no expiry, and the token you present is what scopes the read. Change
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">event_type</code>
            mid-chain and you silently change what the same cursor returns; run a second chain with
            its own cursor instead.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Sizes and rate.</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">limit</code>
            defaults to 100 events and is capped at 1,000. The endpoint allows 60 requests a minute
            per token; past that it answers <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">429</code>, so poll on an interval
            rather than in a tight loop.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="polling">A poller that keeps its place</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Whatever runs the loop — your SIEM's HTTP input, a log agent, or the script below — it has
        exactly one piece of durable state to get right: the cursor. This is the whole contract in
        one file, with no vendor configuration in it.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="poll-emisar-audit.sh" copy_id="audit-poller"><span class="text-zinc-500">#!/bin/sh</span>
set -eu

API=<span class="text-brand-300">"https://emisar.dev/api/audit"</span>
STATE=<span class="text-brand-300">"$&#123;EMISAR_AUDIT_CURSOR:-./emisar-audit.cursor&#125;"</span>  <span class="text-zinc-500"># the cursor, and nothing else</span>
FILTERS=<span class="text-brand-300">"limit=500"</span>                    <span class="text-zinc-500"># identical for the life of this cursor</span>
BOOTSTRAP=<span class="text-brand-300">"2026-07-01T00:00:00Z"</span>       <span class="text-zinc-500"># used only when there is no cursor yet</span>
INTERVAL=60
SINK=<span class="text-brand-300">"$&#123;EMISAR_AUDIT_SINK:-/usr/local/bin/send-emisar-audit&#125;"</span>

work=$(mktemp -d)
trap <span class="text-brand-300">'rm -rf "$work"'</span> 0 HUP INT TERM

cursor=$(cat <span class="text-brand-300">"$STATE"</span> 2&gt;/dev/null || true)

while :; do
  <span class="text-zinc-500"># `since` bootstraps; every later call resumes from the saved cursor.</span>
  if [ -n <span class="text-brand-300">"$cursor"</span> ]; then query=<span class="text-brand-300">"$FILTERS&amp;cursor=$cursor"</span>
  else query=<span class="text-brand-300">"$FILTERS&amp;since=$BOOTSTRAP"</span>; fi

  <span class="text-zinc-500"># -f so a 401/403/429/5xx is a failure, never an empty page. The cursor</span>
  <span class="text-zinc-500"># does not move on failure, so the next attempt re-reads the same page.</span>
  if ! curl -fsS -D <span class="text-brand-300">"$work/headers"</span> -o <span class="text-brand-300">"$work/page.ndjson"</span> \
       -H <span class="text-brand-300">"Authorization: Bearer $EMISAR_AUDIT_TOKEN"</span> <span class="text-brand-300">"$API?$query"</span>; then
    sleep <span class="text-brand-300">"$INTERVAL"</span>; continue
  fi

  <span class="text-zinc-500"># awk exits successfully when the header is absent, which is a normal empty page.</span>
  next=$(awk <span class="text-brand-300">'tolower($1) == "x-next-cursor:" &#123;gsub("\r", "", $2); print $2; exit&#125;'</span> <span class="text-brand-300">"$work/headers"</span>)
  <span class="text-zinc-500"># No X-Next-Cursor means an empty page: caught up, keep the cursor.</span>
  if [ -z <span class="text-brand-300">"$next"</span> ]; then sleep <span class="text-brand-300">"$INTERVAL"</span>; continue; fi

  <span class="text-zinc-500"># Hand off first, persist second. The sink consumes one page synchronously</span>
  <span class="text-zinc-500"># and exits nonzero on rejection; that leaves the cursor unchanged for retry.</span>
  if ! <span class="text-brand-300">"$SINK"</span> &lt; <span class="text-brand-300">"$work/page.ndjson"</span>; then
    sleep <span class="text-brand-300">"$INTERVAL"</span>; continue
  fi
  cursor=<span class="text-brand-300">"$next"</span>
  printf <span class="text-brand-300">'%s'</span> <span class="text-brand-300">"$cursor"</span> &gt; <span class="text-brand-300">"$STATE.tmp"</span>
  mv <span class="text-brand-300">"$STATE.tmp"</span> <span class="text-brand-300">"$STATE"</span>

  <span class="text-zinc-500"># Link: rel="next" rides only a full page — keep paging while it does.</span>
  grep -qi <span class="text-brand-300">'^link:.*rel="next"'</span> <span class="text-brand-300">"$work/headers"</span> || sleep <span class="text-brand-300">"$INTERVAL"</span>
done</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The token lives in the environment and the cursor lives in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">$STATE</code>, which is what makes a
        credential swap boring: mint the replacement, restart the poller with the new value, watch
        one poll succeed, then revoke the old token. The position is the collector's state, not the
        token's, so it never resets. Rotation mechanics are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/credentials" <> "#audit-tokens"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >rotate and revoke credentials</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="health">Watch the poller itself</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An ingest pipeline that quietly stops is worse than one that never existed, and the two
        failures look identical from the SIEM: no new events. Alert on the poller, not only on what
        it delivers — the age of the newest
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">occurred_at</code>
        you have ingested, the time since the cursor last changed, and any run of failed polls.
        Cross-check against the console, which reads the same events without a cursor: rows visible
        there and absent downstream mean the collector, not emisar, is behind.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Your collector shows up in its own feed.">
        <p>
          Every non-empty export is itself recorded, as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">audit.exported</code>, after the
          page is read. An unfiltered collector will therefore see its own earlier exports on a
          later poll. That is deliberate — the watchers are watched — but it has two consequences.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Never treat <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">audit.exported</code>
          as operator activity: a busy exporter is a busy exporter, not a busy account. Exclude it
          from activity dashboards and keep it for credential monitoring, where an export from an
          unexpected address or at an unexpected hour is the signal.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          And never poll until a page comes back empty. Each read can create the event the next read
          finds. Follow <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Link</code>
          while pages are full, then wait for your interval. Empty is a valid caught-up answer, not
          something the endpoint promises after every drain.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="alerting">What to alert on</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Most of the trail is evidence you read after the fact. These are the classes worth waking
        someone for, because each one either changes what is allowed or says a control was tested:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The rules changed.</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">policy.updated</code>
            carries its exact diff, and pack-trust transitions mean new executable bytes were
            approved. Both widen what an agent may do, and both are rare enough to review every
            time.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authority moved.</strong>
            Credentials minted or revoked, role changes, runner enrollment and deletion, and
            identity events —
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">user.mfa_reset_by_admin</code>
            on an account you weren't expecting is the classic one.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Something was refused.</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">action_run.denied</code>
            with its reason, and on the runner side
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">validation_failed</code>
            / <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">action_blocked_by_admission</code>. One
            is noise; a repeated pattern from one actor is an agent probing for what it can't have,
            and it is the most interesting signal you'll get.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A decision waited too long.</strong>
            Approval requests that sit unanswered are an operational signal, not a security one, but
            they are the reason a run "did nothing" — see <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >policies &amp; approvals</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="retention">Retention</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Retention follows your plan — 7 days on Free, 90 on Team, 365 on Enterprise. Each event's
        horizon is fixed when it's written, so a plan downgrade only shortens the window for
        <em>new</em>
        events; it never retroactively deletes history. Both the plan change and every retention
        prune are themselves recorded in the log. Ingest ahead of the window and how long the
        evidence lives becomes your SIEM's policy rather than ours — which is the practical reason
        to fix a stalled poller before the oldest un-ingested event ages out.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="journal">The runner-side journal</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Independently of the cloud, every runner writes a JSONL line per action attempt to
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/var/log/emisar/events.jsonl</code>
        — argv hashes, exit codes, redaction counts, output digests — each line chained to the
        previous by SHA-256.
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar audit verify --all</code>
        walks the chain (including rotated files) and flags a break. Verification covers the
        retained journal or retained suffix; it cannot prove that a privileged host operator did
        not replace or truncate the entire local journal. The record that cannot be changed from
        the host is the <strong>cloud audit</strong>
        — it's off-host and append-only.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        So carry both into your SIEM: ship the host journal through the log pipeline you already run
        on that host, ingest the cloud audit through the export API, and correlate corresponding
        execution attempts on run id and UTC time. A mismatch is an investigation signal, not a
        verdict: first allow for the collector's poll interval and for a host crash before its local
        write, then investigate unexplained gaps as possible loss or tampering. Hiding an action
        from both records still requires changing two independent views, with the cloud copy out of
        reach from the host.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
